<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ussher Fort</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1649</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Fort Crèvecoeur, Fort Crevecoeur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ussher Fort is a fort in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a>, Ghana, built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> in 1649 as Fort Crèvecoeur. It was a base for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> trade, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, near the English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-fort">James Fort</a> and the Danish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osu-castle">Christiansborg</a>. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the exchange of forts of 1868 and was renamed after the colonial administrator Herbert Taylor Ussher.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ussher-fort.jpg" alt="Ussher Fort" width="320" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Twi inaccr · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ussher_Fort_in_Accra_Ghana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
